---
import Plain from '@/layouts/Plain.astro';

/**
 * Privacy. Written against what the code actually does, clause by clause, because a
 * privacy page is a promise and this project's whole argument is that it keeps them.
 * Where something is not built yet, it says so rather than implying it.
 */
const section = 'mt-6 space-y-2';
const heading = 'text-base font-semibold';
const list = 'list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm';
---

<Plain title="Privacy — noka" theme={Astro.locals.theme}>
  <h1 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
    noka is an emergency contact card. This page describes exactly what the service stores, and
    what it deliberately does not.
  </p>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>1. Who is responsible</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      noka is operated by <strong>No More Names Studio</strong> (legal entity to be formalized —
      operator details are kept minimal to protect the operator's privacy). Privacy questions and
      requests: <a href="mailto:hey@lostsignals.studio">hey@lostsignals.studio</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>2. What is stored</h2>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>
        <strong>Your account:</strong> name, email address and an Argon2id password hash. The email
        is the one field stored in plain text, because that is how sign-in finds you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your contacts and notes:</strong> name, relation, phone number, the services that
        number is on, the languages they speak, free-text notes, and whether a contact cannot speak
        or hear. All of it is encrypted with AES-256-GCM before it reaches the database, and the
        key is held in the application environment — never in the database, never in a backup.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your card:</strong> the link slug, a hash of the six-digit PIN, and an encrypted copy
        of the PIN so you can reprint the card. The card's language set.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Scan attempts:</strong> when someone enters a PIN on a card page, a row is written
        with whether it succeeded and an HMAC of the network prefix (the full address for IPv4, the
        /64 for IPv6). The raw address is never stored, and the page views themselves are not
        logged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cookies:</strong> one session cookie when you are signed in, one short-lived cookie
        for the card page you unlocked, one for your language choice, and one for your light/dark
        preference. No advertising or analytics cookies exist.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>3. What is not done</h2>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>No analytics, no pixels, no third-party scripts — on the card page, nothing external is loaded at all.</li>
      <li>No data is sold, shared, brokered or aggregated. Not raw, not anonymised.</li>
      <li>No profiling, no advertising, and no use of your contacts for anything except showing them to someone who has the card and the PIN.</li>
      <li>Users are never charged, so no payment data is collected.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>4. Why it is processed</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      To provide the service you asked for (your account, your card, and the page it opens), and to
      protect it against abuse — the scan-attempt rows exist so a stolen card cannot be
      brute-forced.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>5. Retention</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Scan attempts are deleted 30 days after they are written. The sweep that does it exists and is
      tested — <code class="font-mono text-xs">pnpm purge</code>, with a dry-run that says what it
      would delete — but the schedule that calls it is on the launch checklist rather than running
      today, so read that sentence as the promise it is and not as a running job. Contacts, notes
      and the card stay for as long as your account exists, and are deleted with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>6. Your rights, without asking anyone</h2>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>
        <strong>Download:</strong> Settings → "Download my data" saves everything in this account —
        contacts decrypted, notes, the card and its PIN — as one JSON file. No email, no request, no
        waiting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delete:</strong> Settings → "Delete this account" erases the account, the contacts,
        the notes, the card and its scan history immediately and permanently, and signs you out.
        There is no grace period and no copy kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Change:</strong> contacts, notes, the card and the PIN are yours to edit or reissue at
        any time from the dashboard.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>7. Hosting</h2>
    <p class="text-sm">
      The service is not deployed publicly yet. When it is, it runs on a single hosting provider
      with a managed Postgres database in the same region, and this section will name them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-8 border-t border-line pt-4 text-xs text-fg-muted">
    This page describes the current build and is written to be accurate rather than complete. It is
    not legal advice.
  </p>
</Plain>
